TRANSITIVE INVENTORY (v0.1.0)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The complete resolved dependency closure was audited, not only the direct
dependencies above.

Node runtime closure — 156 packages, resolved from `package-lock.json`:

  119  MIT
   13  Apache-2.0
   13  BSD-3-Clause
    7  ISC
    4  BSD-2-Clause
    0  copyleft (GPL, AGPL, LGPL, MPL, EPL, CDDL, SSPL)
    0  unknown or missing licence declaration

Flutter / Dart closure — 28 packages locked in `apps/console/pubspec.lock`:

   22  BSD-3-Clause (the Dart project default)
    3  MIT (provider, nested, cupertino_icons)
    3  Apache-2.0 (clock, material_color_utilities, fake_async)

`flutter build web` additionally emits `assets/NOTICES`, the Flutter engine's
own consolidated notice file. Within it, every component that ships in a web
build is permissively licensed. The single reciprocal licence it names is the
Mozilla Public License 2.0 on `fallback_root_certificates`, which is certificate
*data* used by the Dart TLS stack; a web build does not include it, because
browser builds delegate TLS to the host browser. It is therefore not
redistributed by this project. The MPL text also *defines* the GNU GPL as a
possible "Secondary License", which is a definition inside MPL-2.0 and not a
GPL-licensed component.

NOTICE-retention obligations: none outstanding. Of the Apache-2.0 packages in
the closure, none distributes a `NOTICE` file, so section 4(d) retention does not
arise for any of them. `LICENSE` files are retained by each distribution.

No third-party component in this dependency set is copyleft in a way that would
require this project to be relicensed.

